WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.892
A dashboard is useful only when its data and services are dependable.

00:00:04.892 --> 00:00:08.886
Health monitoring should cover collectors, queues, databases, storage, frontend services,

00:00:08.886 --> 00:00:11.282
clocks, notification channels, and upstream sources.

00:00:11.282 --> 00:00:17.272
Show stale data and failed ingestion clearly rather than presenting an apparently normal empty view.

00:00:17.272 --> 00:00:19.668
Track disk capacity before storage fills.

00:00:19.668 --> 00:00:22.464
Video and verbose logs can grow rapidly,

00:00:22.464 --> 00:00:26.457
and a full disk may stop ingestion or damage databases.

00:00:26.457 --> 00:00:31.250
Use capacity thresholds, retention enforcement, and protected free space for critical services.

00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:34.844
Monitor queue depth and processing delay to detect overload.

00:00:34.844 --> 00:00:40.035
Back up configuration, event data that must be retained, encryption material where appropriate,

00:00:40.035 --> 00:00:42.032
and documentation needed for recovery.

00:00:42.032 --> 00:00:46.026
A backup is not complete until restoration has been tested.

00:00:46.026 --> 00:00:50.019
Define recovery order, dependencies, responsible owners, and expected recovery time.

00:00:50.019 --> 00:00:53.214
Alert delivery can fail even when detection succeeds.

00:00:53.214 --> 00:00:54.812
Monitor email, messaging, mobile,

00:00:54.812 --> 00:00:59.604
or local notification paths and provide a visible queue for undelivered alerts.

00:00:59.604 --> 00:01:05.994
Important workflows need fallback contacts or local procedures instead of assuming one channel will always work.

00:01:05.994 --> 00:01:07.591
Plan for offline operation.

00:01:07.591 --> 00:01:11.585
Local collection and review may continue during an internet outage,

00:01:11.585 --> 00:01:14.380
while external enrichment and remote notifications pause.

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:17.974
Document which functions degrade, how queued work is handled,

00:01:17.974 --> 00:01:21.169
and how the system reconciles after connectivity returns.

00:01:21.169 --> 00:01:24.763
Maintenance includes patching, certificate renewal, account review, database care,

00:01:24.763 --> 00:01:27.958
model or rule updates, and periodic recovery exercises.

00:01:27.958 --> 00:01:33.150
Schedule changes, preserve rollback options, and verify that data still flows after updates.

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:36.744
Reliability is built through routine observation and practiced procedures,

00:01:36.744 --> 00:01:39.140
not assumed from a successful installation.
